// Package greeting is the bounded context's facade — the package a
// deployment unit imports to assemble it. A *peer* context imports
// userside/service instead: this package names nothing a foreign
// package could write down, which is deliberate and is exactly why
// the seam exists.
//
// It exports factories and NOTHING else. There is deliberately no
// `type Greeter = domain.Greeter` and no `var ErrEmptyName =
// domain.ErrEmptyName` here, however convenient they would be: the
// domain package sits behind this context's internal/, so a consumer
// that cannot name its types cannot declare its own implementation of
// this context's ports either. That turns "only the owning context
// implements its ports" from a review comment into a compile error.
//
// The assembly loses nothing by it. `greeter := greeting.NewGreeter()`
// infers a type main.go is not allowed to write down, and hands it
// straight to the adapter that can.
package greeting

import "<%= domainImport %>"

// NewGreeter assembles the greet use case and returns it behind its
// driving port. Each cmd/ main wires it by hand — constructor
// injection, no framework.
func NewGreeter() domain.Greeter {
	return domain.NewGreeter()
}
